WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:06.000
How well does the sequel to 2017's It adapt
Stephen King's classic novel while tying up

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.920
all the loose ends from the first installment?

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.500
Let's roll up our sleeves, wade into the sewers
and dig into the details of how the saga ends.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.900
This is the ending of It: Chapter Two explained.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.620
And before you open the door labeled "very
scary," be warned: Spoilers await you.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.320
Stephen King's novel It isn't just the story
of a creepy clown terrorizing some kids and

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.620
then returning to do it all over again when
they're adults.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:36.280
There's a whole cosmology to the saga, which
includes a giant, godlike turtle that coughed

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.460
up the known universe when it had a tummy
ache.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:44.440
Aside from a few subtle nods here and there,
the movies mostly sidestep the ancient, interdimensional

00:00:44.449 --> 00:00:50.269
mythology of the book, but one element does
show up in It: Chapter Two - the true form

00:00:50.269 --> 00:00:50.760
of It.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.920
When he goes to visit the Shokopiwah tribe
and takes one of their vision-granting concoctions,

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:02.240
Mike learns that It came to Earth in a meteor
strike that left a crater in Derry, and is

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.780
made up of "Deadlights," mostly orange but
sometimes-blue spheres of light of immense

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.360
power.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.090
Though the lights can be seen in brief moments
in the first film, It appears entirely as

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:19.290
deadlights as Chapter Two reaches its climax,
and even after taking on other forms, the

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.810
deadlights are still visibly powering the
creature, becoming increasingly weaker as

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.340
the Losers literally bring It down to size.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.480
It's true form is revealed because the Losers
perform the Ritual of Chüd, a ceremony that

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.720
differs pretty wildly in the movie from the
way it works in the book.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.960
The movie's take on the ritual involves each
member of the club burning a "token" of his

00:01:40.969 --> 00:01:45.990
or her childhood and reciting a chant to make
the Deadlights turn dark, then trapping the

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.480
Deadlights inside a pyramid-shaped relic Mike
stole.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:55.380
The ritual is the key to defeating It in the
book, though it takes two tries 27 years apart.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.920
But the Losers' attempt essentially fails
in the movie, at least at first.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:04.679
That's because it's revealed Mike lied to
everyone in an attempt to simply bring them

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:05.860
all back together.

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:10.440
The ritual wasn't successful before when the
Native Americans tried to do it, and it doesn't

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.900
kill It when the Losers try this time, either.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.720
At least, not until they've gone through the
wringer just a little bit more so they can

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.660
truly overcome the fears that feed It.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.580
Richie isn't exactly a tough nut to crack.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:25.720
"All right, Rich.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:26.920
What are you afraid of?"

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.180
"Clowns."

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.040
His token for the Ritual of Chüd is a literal
token from the local arcade.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:41.000
And, as is so often the case in the transition
from childhood to adulthood, his fear has

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.480
morphed into anxiety.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.950
The adult Richie throws up twice in It: Chapter
Two and threatens to leave Derry several times

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:52.940
because he just can't stand the idea of losing
what he's gained as a famous stand-up comedian.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:58.180
Richie spends the first half of Chapter Two
mercilessly needling his childhood pal Eddie,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.480
and he doesn't hesitate to call Stanley the
weakest of the Losers when he finds out about

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:03.660
Stanley killing himself.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.860
He can be mean, and has a hard time expressing
his real feelings.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:14.080
Richie has to visit the synagogue where Stanley
had a disastrous bar mitzvah, and more significantly,

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:18.230
come to openly care about the well-being of
his closest friend Eddie, whom he finally

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:23.340
can admit he loves, to overcome his self-absorption
and play a part in defeating It.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.000
For Eddie's part, the fear he has to overcome
is also a sort of self-obsession.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.160
His hypochondria and fear of bodily harm are
paralyzing.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.700
They hamper him from taking action both as
a child and as an adult, like when he's confronted

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.210
with Stanley's bug-like severed head or trying
to rescue his mother from the leper he keeps

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.240
seeing It as.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:50.600
It isn't until he gives up one of his "gazebos"
- his malapropism for "placebo" - in the form

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:54.841
of his inhaler for his token and comes to
believe in the monster-killing power of a

00:03:54.841 --> 00:03:59.810
makeshift spear fashioned from a metal fencepost
that he can work up the courage to mount a

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:04.730
full frontal attack on It, weakening the monster
before the rest of the Losers can deal their

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:06.760
final psychological blows.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.920
Eddie is fatally impaled, but in the service
of saving his friends.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:16.040
After a lifetime of being frozen with fear,
his final act is one of bravery.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.820
Even nearly 30 years after his brother Georgie's
death, Bill is still plagued by guilt and

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.280
feelings of responsibility.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:29.240
He finds himself shouting down a storm drain
just like he did in his youth, and he conveniently

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:34.020
gets Georgie's paper boat back from Pennywise
to use as his token in the ritual.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.980
He fears not being able to save the people
around him.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:42.320
Eventually he tries - and fails - to save
another young Derry boy from suffering a fate

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.740
similar to Georgie's at the end of Pennywise's
teeth.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:51.180
It takes a full-on envisioned confrontation
with his younger self during the final showdown

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:56.310
with It for Bill to come to terms with what
happened and absolve himself of blame, telling

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:59.220
himself that he was, in fact, a good older
brother.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.780
With that resolved, he can help destroy It,
and maybe finally write a decent ending to

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:05.780
his next novel.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:11.440
Though Ben has grown from a chubby, bullied
kid into a handsome, successful man, he still

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.160
clearly feels ostracized.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:19.060
He notably keeps himself at a remove from
his co-workers at his architecture firm, videoconferencing

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.220
in from his massive, empty house.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:26.220
Meanwhile, Beverly is in an abusive marriage
that mirrors her relationship with her father.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:31.720
When she returns to her childhood home, it's
unfamiliar and inhabited by a woman who turns

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.580
into a rampaging, unclothed monster.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:37.120
Nowhere really feels like home to her.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:43.100
After some amnesia-based uncertainty about
who really loved whom on Beverly's part, Beverly

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:47.740
and Ben finally find the linchpins to work
through their fears: each other.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.280
Beverly brings the postcard with the poem
Ben wrote for her to the Ritual of Chüd,

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.880
and Ben brings the yearbook page Bev and no
one else signed.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:00.520
As Ben is made to think he's being buried
alive in the Losers' secret hideout and Bev

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.850
finds herself drowning in blood in the bathroom
stall where she was tormented by bullies,

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:08.220
they reach out to each other and break free
of It's spell.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:13.780
Much like his parents found themselves trapped
as a raging fire killed them, Mike has trapped

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:17.720
himself in Derry while everyone else left
and forgot all about what happened there in

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:18.720
1989.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:20.740
"The farther away, the hazier it all gets.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.160
But me, I never left.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:25.560
So yeah.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:28.500
I remember all of it."

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:34.280
He has appointed himself the chronicler and
expert on all things It, convening with the

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:38.790
Shokopiwah in hopes of unleashing ancient
secrets in time for the returning Losers to

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.060
defeat the creature.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.760
And it works - just not the way he originally
suspected.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.860
More than the Ritual of Chüd, one little
proverb about living things having to abide

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:53.580
by the rules of the shape they inhabit ends
up being the key to taking down Pennywise.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:58.680
When Mike remembers that detail, he sets off
a chain of events that leads all the other

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.720
Losers to bring the monster down to size with
their words, calling It little more than a

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:04.220
clown.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.340
Their name-calling shrinks Pennywise down
so small that they can easily pull out his

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.100
still-beating heart and destroy it.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:17.500
Mike thrives as someone who rallies his friends,
as signified by the way he brings a rock Beverly

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.440
threw at the town bullies as his token.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:25.440
A major difference from the book's ending
is that all the movie Losers leave Derry with

00:07:25.449 --> 00:07:31.050
the memories of their encounter with It intact
- but to everyone's surprise, without the

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:35.250
literal scars from when they cut their hands
to signify their blood bond at the end of

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:36.060
the first movie.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.900
The pain is exorcised.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:41.280
Bill writes a book that's very similar to
Stephen King's It.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:46.700
Ben and Beverly go on boat excursions together,
having finally found true companionship.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:52.200
Richie finishes carving his and Eddie's initials
into a fence, finally revealing how much he

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:52.700
cares.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.420
Mike leaves town at last.

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:58.660
And everyone receives a letter from Stanley,
written before he killed himself.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:03.560
In the letter, Stanley gives a rationale for
his decision to kill himself: Not to escape

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:08.639
facing down It, but to prevent his friends
from dying because he wasn't sure he could

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:10.120
take another round with the creature.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.540
He knew if the Losers weren't unified, they'd
all die.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:18.160
When the adult losers look in a shop window
and see the reflections of their younger selves,

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:19.560
Stanley's there.

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:21.800
"I never felt like a loser when I was with
all of you."

